        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Agincourt</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">25 October 1415 (Saint Crispin's Day)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near Azincourt in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on open land between the woods of Tramecourt and Azincourt; the exact site is disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">English army under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> V, about 9,000 men at the march from Harfleur, of whom about 1,500 men-at-arms and 7,000 longbowmen were deployed; French army under Constable Charles I d'Albret, 14,000 to 15,000 (10,000 men-at-arms and 4,000 to 5,000 other troops). Historians dispute both sets of figures.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A large French force was defeated: around 6,000 French fighting men were killed and 700 to 2,200 taken prisoner, against English losses stated variously from 112 to 1,600. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 November.</span></div>
