    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The English, weakened by disease and short of food after the siege of Harfleur, were blocked on the march to Calais and fought on ground chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a>, a narrow, muddy field between woods.</li><li>Historians disagree about the numbers: Anne Curry's study of administrative records gives the French and English in proportions of about four to three, while others hold that the English were outnumbered several times over.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> ordered the killing of most French prisoners at some point in the battle, fearing a French return to the fight, while sparing the highest ranked for ransom.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Burgundian sources have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> telling his men that the French had boasted of cutting two fingers from the right hand of every archer. Whether this was true is open to question and is still debated.</p></aside>
    </section>

