    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1414</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> V's negotiations with the French over his claims and a marriage to Catherine, daughter of Charles VI, break down</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text">On 13 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a>'s army lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and besieges Harfleur, which surrenders on 22 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text">The English leave Harfleur on 8 October and march towards Calais, forced south by French blocking of the Somme</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text">On 24 October the two armies face each other but the French decline battle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text">On 25 October the English advance, the French cavalry charge fails and the French men-at-arms are defeated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> orders most of the French prisoners killed, sparing only the highest ranked</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 November and is received in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on the 23rd</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1415</span><span class="rf-text">Within ten days of the battle the truce between the Armagnacs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> breaks down, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> eighteen months to prepare a new campaign</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-agincourt" data-v="5ca3cf4637" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies are drawn up</b> The English stood in three groups across the defile, with archers on the flanks and men-at-arms in the centre. The French stood in a vanguard and a main battle behind it, with dismounted men-at-arms in the wings, cavalry on the flanks of the vanguard and a mounted rearguard.</li><li><b>The English move up and reset their stakes</b> Henry ordered his army forward and the stakes were pulled out of the ground, carried to the new position and set again to protect the archers.</li><li><b>The longbowmen shoot from extreme range</b> The English longbowmen began to shoot at the French from extreme range, about 300 yards. The French cavalry did not charge until after this first volley.</li><li><b>The French cavalry charge the archers</b> The French cavalry, disorganised and not at full numbers, charged towards the longbowmen. They could not outflank the archers because of the woodland, and could not charge through the stakes.</li><li><b>The wounded horses break back through the French</b> The charge failed and the cavalry fell back. Wounded and panicking horses galloped through the advancing infantry, scattering and trampling it, while the vanguard walked on through the mud.</li><li><b>The French men-at-arms reach the English line</b> The surviving French men-at-arms reached the front of the English line and pushed it back, while the longbowmen on the flanks went on shooting, now at close range.</li><li><b>The archers drop their bows and attack</b> When their arrows ran out the archers dropped their bows and attacked the disordered, tired and wounded French men-at-arms with hatchets, swords, spears and the mallets they had used to drive in their stakes.</li><li><b>The second line joins and the fight is lost in the press</b> The French second line joined the attack but was swallowed up, the narrow ground hampering it. The fighting lasted about three hours, until the leaders of the second line were killed or captured, as those of the first line had been.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war.jpg" alt="Hundred Years' War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Azincourt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Battle of Agincourt belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> (1337 to 1453). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> V claimed the title of King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through his great-grandfather Edward III, although English kings had generally been ready to renounce it if the French recognised the English claim to Aquitaine and other lands under the terms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a>. In 1414 Henry offered to give up the claim if the French paid the 1.6 million crowns outstanding from the ransom of John II, captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356, and conceded Anjou, Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Normandy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and Aquitaine. He would also marry Charles VI's daughter Catherine with a dowry of 2 million crowns. The French offered a dowry of 600,000 crowns and an enlarged Aquitaine. The English felt the French had mocked their claims, and on 19 April 1415 the Great Council agreed to war.</p><p>Henry's army landed on 13 August 1415. The army of about 12,000 men and up to 20,000 horses besieged Harfleur, which surrendered on 22 September, and the army did not leave until 8 October. It had suffered many casualties from disease. Rather than retire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Henry decided to march most of his army, roughly 9,000, through Normandy to Calais, to show that his right to rule in the duchy was more than an abstract claim, and as a provocation to the dauphin, who had not answered his challenge to personal combat at Harfleur. The French moved to block the Somme and forced Henry south to find a ford, which the English crossed south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a>. By 24 October both armies faced each other, but the French declined battle, hoping for more troops.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Agincourt&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Christopher Hibbert, Great Battles – Agincourt (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1971)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the morning of 25 October <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> deployed his army, approximately 1,500 men-at-arms and 7,000 longbowmen, across a 750-yard (690 m) part of the defile. English and Welsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> made up nearly 80 percent of the army. The king led the centre, Edward, Duke of York the right wing and Baron Thomas Camoys the left, and Sir Thomas Erpingham commanded the archers. The archers drove pointed stakes into the ground at an angle to turn aside cavalry. The English had very little food, had marched 260 miles (420 km) in two and a half weeks and were suffering from dysentery.</p><p>King Charles VI did not command the French army because he suffered from psychotic illnesses. It was led by Constable Charles I d'Albret and noblemen of the Armagnac party. The French are counted at 10,000 men-at-arms and some 4,000 to 5,000 miscellaneous foot soldiers (archers, crossbowmen and shield-bearers), totalling 14,000 to 15,000, and each man-at-arms was probably accompanied by an armed servant, which some historians leave out of the count. The French formed a vanguard of 4,800 men-at-arms and a main battle of 3,000, with 600 dismounted men-at-arms on each wing. A cavalry force of 800 to 1,200, under Clignet de Bréban and Louis de Bosredon, was to break the English archers, and some 200 mounted men were to attack the English rear. Many lords demanded places at the front for glory and ransoms, so the bulk of the men-at-arms were massed in front and the archers and crossbowmen were placed behind them. The rearguard on horseback was leaderless.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Agincourt&#8221; cites where it covers English deployment, French deployment, Numbers at Agincourt:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War IV: Cursed Kings (Faber &amp; Faber, 2015)</li><li>Anne Curry, The Battle of Agincourt: Sources and Interpretations (Boydell Press, 2000)</li><li>Anne Curry, Agincourt: A New History (Tempus, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england.jpg" alt="Henry V of England" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_School,_16th_century_-_Henry_V_(1387-1422)_-_RCIN_403443_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For three hours after sunrise there was no fighting. The French were expecting thousands more men and blocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a>'s retreat. Henry had to halt the retreat and engage them before the French were reinforced, so his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> pulled up their stakes, carried them forward and set them again. The French cavalry, disorganised and not at full numbers, charged only after the English began shooting. It could not outflank the archers because of the woodland nor pass the stakes. John Keegan argues that the longbows' main effect was to injure horses, which galloped back through the advancing infantry.</p><p>The French men-at-arms advanced through recently ploughed, muddy ground, with visors down against the arrows, in armour weighing 50 to 60 pounds (23 to 27 kg). Modern historians are divided on how well longbows pierced plate armour of the time. The French reached the English line and pushed it back at first, but became so tightly packed that men in the third rank could hardly use their swords. Knights who fell found it hard to rise. When the archers ran out of arrows they dropped their bows and attacked with hatchets, swords and mallets. The fighting lasted about three hours, and the leaders of the second line were killed or captured as those of the first had been. English accounts say Henry fought hand to hand and stood over his wounded brother Humphrey, receiving an axe blow to the head. Ysembart d'Azincourt, with a small number of men-at-arms and about 600 peasants, attacked the English baggage train and took some of Henry's treasures.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Agincourt&#8221; cites where it covers Opening moves, French cavalry attack, Main French assault, Attack on the English baggage train:</p><ul><li>John Keegan, The Face of Battle: A Study of Agincourt, Waterloo, and the Somme (Viking Adult, 1976)</li><li>Anne Curry, The Battle of Agincourt: Sources and Interpretations (Boydell Press, 2000)</li><li>Anne Curry, Agincourt: A New History (Tempus, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At some point after the first victory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> became alarmed that the French were regrouping and ordered most of the French prisoners killed, possibly thousands, sparing only the highest ranked, who were most likely to fetch a ransom. Most chroniclers say he feared that the prisoners, who outnumbered their captors, would rearm from the weapons on the field. Contemporary chroniclers did not criticise him. The English knights refused to take part because they thought it unchivalrous. Keegan argues that the aim was to terrorise the prisoners and that relatively few were killed before the French reserves fled and Henry rescinded the order.</p><p>Around 6,000 French fighting men were killed, among them 90 to 120 lords and bannerets, including the dukes of Alençon, Bar and Brabant, nine counts and the Constable d'Albret. According to the heralds 3,069 knights and squires died and at least 2,600 more bodies lacked coats of arms. Estimates of prisoners vary between 700 and 2,200, among them the dukes of Orléans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. For the English, records identify at least 112 killed, Monstrelet reported 600, and Jean de Wavrin 1,600 of all ranks; the dead included the Duke of York. Henry returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 November and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on the 23rd. Within ten days the Armagnac and Burgundian factions broke their truce and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, giving Henry eighteen months to prepare.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Agincourt&#8221; cites where it covers Henry executes French prisoners, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Anne Curry, The Battle of Agincourt: Sources and Interpretations (Boydell Press, 2000)</li><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War IV: Cursed Kings (Faber &amp; Faber, 2015)</li><li>John Keegan, The Face of Battle: A Study of Agincourt, Waterloo, and the Somme (Viking Adult, 1976)</li><li>Anne Curry, Agincourt: A New History (Tempus, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is documented by at least seven contemporary accounts, three from eyewitnesses. Jean Le Fèvre de Saint-Remy, a Burgundian who was present, and the anonymous Gesta Henrici Quinti, believed to be by a chaplain in the king's household who would have been in the baggage train, are eyewitnesses; Enguerrand de Monstrelet, also Burgundian, is the other frequently used account, and the monk of Saint-Denis is quoted for the French side. After the battle the heralds of both armies, with Montjoie, settled the name Azincourt. Curry and Mortimer question the Gesta's reliability, noting doubts about how far it was written as propaganda for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> V, and Mortimer thinks it overstates both French numbers and English losses at Harfleur.</p><p>The site is uncertain. It may lie on the strip between Tramecourt and Azincourt, but the lack of archaeological evidence there has led to suggestions of a site to the west, a case Michael Livingston made in 2019. The numbers are disputed. Most primary sources have the English outnumbered several times. Anne Curry, from administrative records, argued for 12,000 French and 9,000 English; Juliet Barker, Jonathan Sumption and Clifford Rogers say the English probably had 6,000 (5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and 900 to 1,000 men-at-arms) and that Curry's records are incomplete. Ian Mortimer calls 15,000 French against 8,000 to 9,000 English the most extreme imbalance that is credible. Rogers reckons the French at 24,000 counting armed servants; Barker holds that the English were outnumbered at least four to one. Historians disagree less about French numbers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Agincourt&#8221; cites where it covers Contemporary accounts:</p><ul><li>John Keegan, The Face of Battle: A Study of Agincourt, Waterloo, and the Somme (Viking Adult, 1976)</li><li>Anne Curry, The Battle of Agincourt: Sources and Interpretations (Boydell Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy.jpg" alt="Battle of Crécy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_crecy_froissart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Loyset Liédet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agincourt is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> among the English victories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. It began a period of English dominance that lasted until 1429 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">Siege of Orléans</a>. The victory was militarily decisive, but its impact was complex. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> returned a conquering hero, seen as blessed by God in the eyes of his subjects and European powers outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The victory established the legitimacy of the Lancastrian monarchy and of his later campaigns, and the damage to Normandy's political and military structures made them easier. The French suffered a catastrophic defeat; entire noble families were wiped out in the male line, and Barker writes that the battle cut a great swath through French society in Artois, Ponthieu, Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>.</p><p>Songs followed soon, the best known the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agincourt-carol">Agincourt Carol</a> of the first half of the 15th century. The best-known depiction is Shakespeare's Henry V (1599), whose St Crispin's Day speech has Henry urging a band of brothers. Shakespeare's casualty figures, 10,000 French and fewer than thirty English, are not historical, and the play shows Henry's order to kill prisoners as a break with chivalry. Films from 1944, 1989 and 2019 draw on the play. The battle was likened to the British Expeditionary Force's stand at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. A museum, the Centre Azincourt 1415, stands in the village.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/agincourt-carol">Agincourt Carol<span>c. 1415</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 25 October 1415 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry</a> V's weakened army, blocked on its way to Calais, defeated a larger French army in a narrow, muddy field near Azincourt. The French lost thousands of fighting men and many of their leaders, and the numbers on each side are still disputed.</em></p>

